\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces This scheme illustrates the essential steps involved in a Molecular Dynamics (MD) simulation. The process begins with the definition of the initial state of the system, including the atomic coordinates and velocities, the selection of an appropriate force field, and the specification of the simulation conditions. The force field is then employed to compute the forces acting on each particle, which enables the time evolution of the system through the numerical integration of the equations of motion. At each time step, additional algorithms are applied to ensure that the system evolves under the desired thermodynamic conditions, such as constant temperature and/or pressure. This includes, for instance, the use of thermostats and barostats to control the corresponding ensemble. The propagation of positions and velocities, together with the enforcement of these conditions, is repeated iteratively over successive time steps. The simulation proceeds until a predefined convergence criterion is met or the maximum number of integration steps is reached.}}{49}{figure.caption.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:MDFlow}{{3.1}{49}{This scheme illustrates the essential steps involved in a Molecular Dynamics (MD) simulation. The process begins with the definition of the initial state of the system, including the atomic coordinates and velocities, the selection of an appropriate force field, and the specification of the simulation conditions. The force field is then employed to compute the forces acting on each particle, which enables the time evolution of the system through the numerical integration of the equations of motion. At each time step, additional algorithms are applied to ensure that the system evolves under the desired thermodynamic conditions, such as constant temperature and/or pressure. This includes, for instance, the use of thermostats and barostats to control the corresponding ensemble. The propagation of positions and velocities, together with the enforcement of these conditions, is repeated iteratively over successive time steps. The simulation proceeds until a predefined convergence criterion is met or the maximum number of integration steps is reached}{figure.caption.4}{}}
\newlabel{eq:integral_v}{{3.3}{49}{The Physics Behind Molecular Dynamics}{equation.3.3}{}}
\newlabel{eq:integral_r}{{3.4}{49}{The Physics Behind Molecular Dynamics}{equation.3.4}{}}
\newlabel{eq:taylor_forward}{{3.5}{50}{The Physics Behind Molecular Dynamics}{equation.3.5}{}}
\newlabel{eq:taylor_backward}{{3.6}{50}{The Physics Behind Molecular Dynamics}{equation.3.6}{}}
\newlabel{eq:verlet}{{3.7}{50}{The Physics Behind Molecular Dynamics}{equation.3.7}{}}
\newlabel{eq:leapfrog}{{3.8}{50}{The Physics Behind Molecular Dynamics}{equation.3.8}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces Scheme depicting the effect of periodic boundary conditions. The image shows how a particle that at an instant \(t\) is displaced outside the original simulation space (black solid line), re-enters into the box from its periodic image at a time \(t + \Delta t\). The number of particles in the simulation cell and in all the periodic images are kept constant. Image generated with Matplotlib.}}{51}{figure.caption.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:PBCs}{{3.2}{51}{Scheme depicting the effect of periodic boundary conditions. The image shows how a particle that at an instant \(t\) is displaced outside the original simulation space (black solid line), re-enters into the box from its periodic image at a time \(t + \Delta t\). The number of particles in the simulation cell and in all the periodic images are kept constant. Image generated with Matplotlib}{figure.caption.5}{}}
